@meta
id: 05-06
title: The WORD, and why capitals matter
chapter: 05
type: lesson
skills: WORD-motions, word-w
par: 36
minutes: 6
vscode: no equivalent — nothing there jumps by whitespace
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Lesson five left you with a problem: `w` stops at every dot and bracket,
so crossing `this.emailService.send(customer.email,` costs eight presses.

The capital does the other thing. `W` moves by *WORD* — a run of
non-blank characters, whatever is in it. Whitespace is the only boundary
it recognises. `this.emailService.send(customer.email,` is one `W` press.

It is the same distinction for every one of these motions. Lower case
respects punctuation; upper case only sees spaces.

The refund email should include the amount. Add `amount: order.total, `
to the payload object so it reads:

    { amount: order.total, orderId: order.id }

Get there with `W`, not `w`. Par assumes you do.

@keys
| `W` | forward to the start of the next WORD (whitespace separated) |
| `w` | forward to the start of the next word (punctuation counts) |
| `i` | insert before the cursor |

@hints
Say the line out loud with spaces as the only breaks. How many jumps is
it really to `orderId:`?
--
`W` stops at: `await`, the whole `this.emailService.send(customer.email,`
chunk, `'order.refunded',`, the `{`, and then `orderId:`. Five.
--
`2j` gets you to the line, `5W` gets you to `orderId:`, then `i`, type
`amount: order.total, ` and `Esc`.

@solution
    2j  5W  i amount: order.total,  <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Five `W` presses. The same trip with `w` is nineteen, because `w` stops
at every dot, paren, quote and comma along the way.

The way to hold it in your head: `w` is for moving *within* an expression
and `W` is for moving *past* one. When you are aiming at a piece of
`order.pricing.subtotal`, `w` is the tool. When that whole thing is just
scenery between you and the argument you care about, `W` is.

This is the single biggest lever in the chapter. A VS Code user has no
whitespace-level jump at all — Ctrl-Right behaves roughly like `w` and
there is nothing coarser, which is exactly why crossing a long chained
call there feels like it needs the mouse.

Both keep working the same way you already learned: `W` lands on the
first character of the WORD, so `W` then `i` inserts in front of it.

@start order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async notifyRefund(order: Order, customer: Customer) {
    await this.emailService.send(customer.email, 'order.refunded', { orderId: order.id });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
export class OrderProvider {
  public async notifyRefund(order: Order, customer: Customer) {
    await this.emailService.send(customer.email, 'order.refunded', { amount: order.total, orderId: order.id });
  }
}
